.Dd $Mdocdate: November 8 2026 $
.Dt AWGCTL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm awgctl
.Nd manage AmneziaWG (awg) network interfaces
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm up
.Ar name
.Pq Oo Fl -config Ar path Oc
.Nm
.Cm down
.Ar name
.Nm
.Cm show
.Oo Fl j Oc
.Oo Ar name Oc
.Nm
.Cm set
.Ar name
.Ar key value ...
.Nm
.Cm add
.Ar name
.Cm peer
.Ar pubkey
.Oo Cm endpoint Ar addr:port Oo
.Oo Cm allowed-ips Ar list Oc
.Oo Cm psk Ar key Oc
.Oo Cm keepalive Ar N Oc
.Nm
.Cm del
.Ar name
.Cm peer
.Ar pubkey
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility configures AmneziaWG interfaces of the
.Xr awg 4
driver.
It is a standalone replacement for the
.Xr ifconfig 8
AmneziaWG keywords: interface creation and destruction shell out to the
stock
.Xr ifconfig 8
.Pq Dv SIOCIFCREATE2
while every other parameter is applied with one
.Dv SIOCSAWG
set or
.Dv SIOCGAWG
get on
.Va struct awg_data_io .
.Pp
The subcommands are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm up Ar name Oo Fl -config Ar path Oc
Read the configuration (default
.Pa /etc/amnezia/amneziawg/Ar name.conf ) ,
create
.Ar name
if it does not exist, apply the whole configuration in a single
.Dv SIOCSAWG ,
replacing the peer list with the file's
.Ic [Peer]
sections,
set any
.Ic Address
entries and
.Ic MTU ,
and bring the interface up.
Applying the same file twice is idempotent.
The address is verified by reading the interface back: the comparison uses
the parsed address bytes, the address family and the prefix length.
A netmask must be a contiguous run of ones, and an entry without a netmask
does not prove an explicitly given prefix, so such an entry is not a match.
An IPv6 scope (the
.Ql % Ar iface
suffix of a link-local address) is not part of the address bytes: it is
stripped before the comparison and ignored.
.It Cm down Ar name
Remove all peers, bring
.Ar name
down and destroy the interface.
.It Cm show Oo Fl j Oc Oo Ar name Oc
Print the current kernel state, one field per line, in a stable order:
.Ic awgstate ,
.Ic awgmtu ,
.Ic awgport ,
.Ic awgrtable ,
.Ic awgpubkey ,
.Ic awgversion ,
.Ic awgjc ,
.Ic awgjmin ,
.Ic awgjmax ,
.Ic awgs1 Ns - Ns Ic awgs4 ,
.Ic awgh1 Ns - Ns Ic awgh4 ,
.Ic awgi1 Ns - Ns Ic awgi5 ,
.Ic awghpk ,
.Ic awgcpa ,
the five timing keys,
.Ic awgrandomtrailers ,
.Ic awgdisablecookies ,
then each peer with its
.Ic awgpeer
public key,
.Ic awgpsk ,
.Ic awgpka ,
.Ic awgendpoint ,
transmit/receive counters, last handshake and
.Ic awgaip
entries.
Only public keys are printed; the private key is never shown.
Without
.Ar name ,
every
.Ic awg Ns Ar N
interface is shown.
.Pp
Every field is read from the kernel:
.Ic awgstate
and
.Ic awgmtu
come from the
.Dv AF_LINK
entry of
.Xr getifaddrs 3
(the kernel's if_data for the interface), everything else from the
.Dv SIOCGAWG
reply.
A field the kernel does not hold is never printed: an unset interface flag
prints nothing, a peer that never handshaked has no handshake line, and an
interface whose if_data the kernel did not hand over prints no MTU.
.Pp
With
.Fl j
the same state is emitted as one JSON document: an array with one object per
interface, each holding one record per peer in its
.Ic peers
array.
Key order is fixed, no ANSI escape is written, and no value is derived from
the clock, so two runs over unchanged kernel state are byte-identical.
The schema is:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[
  {
    "name": "awg0",
    "state": "up",
    "mtu": 1420,
    "awgport": 51820,
    "awgrtable": 0,
    "awgpubkey": "base64...",
    "awgversion": "3.1",
    "awgjc": 10,
    "awgjmin": 5,
    "awgjmax": 15,
    "awgs1": 1,
    "awgs2": 2,
    "awgs3": 3,
    "awgs4": 4,
    "awgh1": "100-400",
    "awgh2": 200,
    "awgh3": 300,
    "awgh4": 400,
    "awgi1": "<b 0xc700000001><r 16><t>",
    "awghpk": true,
    "awgcpa": 40,
    "awgrekeyaftertime": 86400,
    "awgrekeytimeout": 120,
    "awgrejectaftertime": 180,
    "awgkeepalivetimeout": 10,
    "awgmaxhandshakeattempts": 3,
    "awgrandomtrailers": true,
    "awgdisablecookies": true,
    "peers": [
      {
        "awgpeer": "base64...",
        "awgdescr": "",
        "awgpsk": true,
        "awgpka": 25,
        "awgendpoint": "192.0.2.1:51820",
        "tx": 12345,
        "rx": 54321,
        "last_handshake": 1700000042,
        "awgaip": ["10.9.0.2/32", "fd00::2/128"]
      }
    ]
  }
]
.Ed
.Pp
A range is a number when both bounds agree and a
.Dq lo-hi
string otherwise.
The boolean flags
.Ic awghpk ,
.Ic awgrandomtrailers ,
.Ic awgdisablecookies
and
.Ic awgpsk
are emitted as
.Ic true .
.Ic tx
and
.Ic rx
are the kernel's byte counters;
.Ic last_handshake
is the kernel's own timestamp in seconds since the epoch, and is omitted for
a peer that never handshaked.
.Ic awgdescr
and
.Ic awgaip
are always present, as an empty string and an empty array, because the kernel
always holds those fields.
.Pp
A string value is JSON-escaped: quote, backslash,
.Li \eb ,
.Li \ef ,
.Li \en ,
.Li \er
and
.Li \et
become two characters, and any other byte below 0x20 becomes
.Li \eu00XX .
The text form is unescaped: it prints the kernel's bytes. A description
therefore stays readable in both modes and never breaks the document.
With no
.Ar name
the array holds every
.Ic awg Ns Ar N
interface, in the order they are listed.
.It Cm set Ar name Ar key value ...
Apply the given interface keys in one
.Dv SIOCSAWG .
Keys use the configuration file spelling
.Pq Ic PrivateKey , Ic ListenPort , Ic Table , Ic Version , Ic Jc , ...
A value of
.Dq -
reads that value from standard input, which keeps
.Ic PrivateKey
and
.Ic HeaderProtectionKey
out of the argument vector.
Fields the kernel groups (Jc/Jmin/Jmax, S1/S2, H1\-H4) are read back from
the interface first, so a partial set keeps the siblings it does not name.
The read and the write are two separate ioctls, so the contract is
last-writer-wins: a concurrent writer that commits between them is not
detected, and the last
.Dv SIOCSAWG
wins per field group.
The kernel has no generation counter, so no compare-and-swap token is
checked; an unrelated
.Ic set
still writes back only the fields it reads as siblings.
.Pp
.It Cm add Ar name Cm peer Ar pubkey Oo ... Oc
Add (or update) one peer with an endpoint, allowed IPs, preshared key
and persistent keepalive.
A
.Ic psk
of
.Dq -
reads the key from standard input.
.It Cm del Ar name Cm peer Ar pubkey
Remove one peer.
.El
.Ss Interface names
An
.Ar name
is 1 to 15 characters from the set
.Ql [A-Za-z0-9._-]
and must not begin with
.Ql -
or
.Ql .
The limit is
.Dv IFNAMSIZ Ns -1 ,
the width of the field the kernel stores the name in: a longer name would be
truncated and the command would act on a different interface than the one
typed.
A name that does not match is refused as a usage error
.Pq exit 1
before the first ioctl, address change, ifconfig execution or
configuration-file access, so a rejected name has no side effect.
.Ss Arguments
Every subcommand has a fixed argument count and the synopsis is enforced:
a missing argument, a surplus argument and an unknown keyword are usage
errors.
.Cm up
takes
.Fl -config
at most once, and only after
.Ar name ;
a second
.Fl -config
is a usage error.
.Cm set
requires at least one
.Ar key value
pair.
A repeated key is accepted and the last occurrence wins; the pairs are
applied to one
.Dv SIOCSAWG
in the order given, so the last value of a key is the one that is written.
.Cm add
accepts
.Cm allowed-ips
more than once, and the entries accumulate; a repeated
.Cm endpoint ,
.Cm psk
or
.Cm keepalive
replaces the earlier value.
Keywords are case-sensitive and use the spelling shown in the synopsis, not
the configuration file spelling.
.Ss Output and diagnostics
.Cm show
writes the interface fields to standard output, one field per line, and
writes nothing else there.
No other subcommand writes to standard output: a successful
.Cm up ,
.Cm down ,
.Cm set ,
.Cm add
or
.Cm del
is silent unless it warns, and every diagnostic, every warning and the
.Fn usage
text are written to standard error.
The
.Ev AWGCTL_DEBUG
trace is written to standard error as well.
.Ss Unprivileged use
.Nm
is not setuid and needs no privilege to answer a contract error: arguments,
interface names and configuration-file trust are checked before any
privileged operation, and each refusal is a usage error
.Pq exit 1 .
The configuration file is refused, rather than read, when the caller is not
root and the tree holding it is not owned by
.Ic root
.Pq exit 1 .
When the kernel refuses a
.Dv SIOCGAWG
or
.Dv SIOCSAWG
the command reports the kernel's error and exits with status 3, which is how
an interface the caller may not configure is reported
.Pq Er EPERM .
.Ss Configuration file
The file uses the wg-quick
.Ic Key = value
grammar with
.Ic [Interface]
and
.Ic [Peer]
sections and
.Ic #
comments; see
.Xr awg-quick 8 .
Interface keys:
.Ic PrivateKey
(required),
.Ic ListenPort ,
.Ic Address ,
.Ic MTU ,
.Ic DNS ,
.Ic Table ,
.Ic Version
.Pq Ic legacy
or
.Ic 3.1 ,
.Ic Jc ,
.Ic Jmin ,
.Ic Jmax ,
.Ic S1 Ns - Ns Ic S4 ,
.Ic H1 Ns - Ns Ic H4
(single value or
.Va low Ns - Ns Ar high
range),
.Ic I1 Ns - Ns Ic I5 ,
.Ic HeaderProtectionKey ,
.Ic ContentPaddingAddition ,
.Ic RekeyAfterTime ,
.Ic RekeyTimeout ,
.Ic RejectAfterTime ,
.Ic KeepaliveTimeout ,
.Ic MaxHandshakeAttempts ,
.Ic RandomTrailers
.Pq Ic 0 Ns / Ic 1 Ns / Ic on Ns / Ic off
and
.Ic DisableCookies .
Peer keys:
.Ic PublicKey
(required),
.Ic PresharedKey ,
.Ic Endpoint
.Pq Ar host Ns : Ns Ar port
or
.Pq Oo Ar v6 Oc Ns : Ns Ar port ,
resolved with
.Fn getaddrinfo ,
.Ic AllowedIPs
(comma separated) and
.Ic PersistentKeepalive .
An unknown key refuses the whole file.
The file must be mode 0600 (or 0400), root-owned, with root-owned and
not group/other-writable ancestor directories.
The
.Ic DNS
values are parsed but not applied by
.Nm ;
configure name resolution separately.
.Ss Protocol versions
A
.Ic Version
of
.Ic legacy
selects the AmneziaWG < 3.1 parameter set
.Pq Ic Jc Ns / Ns Ic Jmin Ns / Ns Ic Jmax Ns / Ns Ic S1 Ns / Ns Ic S2 Ns / Ns Ic H1 Ns - Ns Ic H4 ;
3.1 adds
.Ic S3 ,
.Ic S4 ,
.Ic I1 Ns - Ns Ic I5 ,
.Ic HeaderProtectionKey ,
.Ic ContentPaddingAddition ,
the five timing keys,
.Ic RandomTrailers
and
.Ic DisableCookies .
Without an explicit
.Ic Version
key, a file that sets any 3.1-only key selects 3.1, otherwise legacy.
.Sh SECRET LIFETIME
The private key, header protection key and preshared key are read into
heap buffers and wiped with
.Fn explicit_bzero
when the parsed configuration is released, which happens on every exit
path, including the
.Fn err
and
.Fn errx
paths that go through
.Fn exit .
A key given as a literal command argument is accepted (with a warning)
and stays in the argument vector, where other local users can see it with
.Xr ps 1
until the kernel reclaims the process area; prefer
.Dq -
with the key on standard input.
Three copies are outside the tool's reach and are not wiped: the kernel's
copy of the key inside the interface structure (it lives until the field
is replaced or the interface is destroyed),
.Fn stdio
buffers used while reading the file, and freed allocator chunks that the
allocator may reuse.
.Sh SYSTEM CALLS
.Nm
calls
.Fn pledge
with
.Dq stdio rpath inet route tty dns proc exec
and
.Fn unveil
on
.Pa /etc/amnezia/amneziawg
(read) and
.Pa /sbin/ifconfig
(execute).
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev AWGCTL_DEBUG
Print the SIOCGAWG size-probe trace (iteration, requested and returned
size, errno) on stderr.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with one of the following values:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
A usage error, or an I/O failure.
A usage error is an unknown subcommand, an argument that does not match the
synopsis
.Pq missing, surplus, repeated where the synopsis allows one, or malformed ,
or an invalid interface name.
An I/O failure includes a configuration file that does not exist, cannot be
opened, or fails the ownership, mode or symlink trust checks below.
.It 2
A configuration error: the file, or a value given to
.Cm set ,
parses as the expected spelling but is not valid for the driver.
.It 3
A
.Dv SIOCSAWG
or
.Dv SIOCGAWG
ioctl failure, including
.Er EPERM
when the caller may not configure the interface.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# awgctl up awg9
# awgctl show awg9
# awgctl set awg9 ListenPort 51820
# awgctl add awg9 peer QVZP... endpoint 192.0.2.1:51820 \\
	allowed-ips 10.0.0.2/32,fd00::2/128 keepalive 25
# awgctl del awg9 peer QVZP...
# awgctl down awg9
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr awg 4 ,
.Xr ifconfig 8 ,
.Xr awg-quick 8
.Rs
.%A Amnezia
.%T AmneziaWG protocol
.Re
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility first appeared in awg-openbsd 1.2 as the
.Dq Option B
management tool of
.Pa docs/ADMIN-INTERFACE.md .
